===== .claude/skills/cdk/SKILL.md =====
---
name: cdk
description: Drive the `cdk` CLI for Cloud DevKit — the shared TPU cluster used for vLLM/SGLang/TorchTPU development. Use for anything involving cdk jobs (create, list, log, exec, attach, delete, desc, tag, sync-outputs), recipes, cluster capacity (`cdk usage`, `cdk queue`), Perfetto/xprof profiling, or debugging why a CDK job is stuck, failing, or unschedulable.
---

# Cloud DevKit (`cdk`)

`cdk` submits and manages jobs on a shared GKE TPU (v7x) cluster used by ~40 engineers.
Everything runs in the `cloud-devkit-jobs` namespace, is admitted by Kueue, and is
described by a **recipe** — a Go-templated Kubernetes JobSet.

```
recipe (JobSet template)  ──cdk job create──>  Kueue queue  ──admission──>  pods on TPU nodes
                                                                                  │
                                          job_manager watches ─── archives logs, generates traces
```

## Mental model

- **Recipe** — a JobSet YAML + Go template, registered in `recipes.yml` at the root of
  **this** repo. `cdk job create <recipe> KEY=VALUE...` renders and submits it.
- **Job** — one submission, id `j-xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx`. A JobSet fans out to
  replicatedJobs → pods → containers, so "one job" is usually several pods.
- **Two independent status fields**, and confusing them is the most common mistake:
  - **JobStatus** — the Kubernetes/Kueue view: `InQueue` → `Pending` → `Running` →
    `Succeeded`/`Failed`, or `Deleted`. This is what `cdk job list` shows by default.
  - **State** — CDK's own post-processing lifecycle: `Active` → `ExecutionComplete` →
    `LogArchiveSucceeded` → `Finalizing` → `Complete` (plus `*Failed` variants). Only
    visible with `cdk job list -o wide` or `cdk job desc`. A job can be `Succeeded` for
    minutes while State is still working through log archiving and trace generation.
- **Kueue** — every user gets a LocalQueue `lq-<$USER with _ → ->`, auto-created against
  cluster queue `tpu7x-cq`. `JobStatus=InQueue` means waiting for admission, not broken.

## Command map

| Goal | Command | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Cluster capacity | `cdk usage [-o json]` | tree of NodePool → Node → Pod → [references/capacity.md](references/capacity.md) |
| Queue / quota pressure | `cdk queue [-o json]` | pending & admitted workloads per user |
| List recipes | `cdk recipe list [-o json]` | name, owner, YAML path |
| Submit | `cdk job create <recipe> [K=V...]` | → [references/jobs.md](references/jobs.md) |
| List jobs | `cdk job list [-a -A -n N -u USER -t TAG -r RECIPE -o wide\|debug\|json]` | |
| Inspect | `cdk job desc <id> --no-color` / `cdk job recipe <id> --no-color` | DB record / rendered JobSet |
| Logs | `cdk job log <id> [-p POD -c CONTAINER] [-f] [-e] [-P]` | `-p`/`-c` only when ambiguous |
| Shell in | `cdk job exec <id> [-p POD -c CONTAINER]` | needs a TTY |
| Attach stdio | `cdk job attach <id> [-p POD -c CONTAINER]` | needs a TTY |
| Kill | `cdk job delete <id> [-f]` | releases the TPUs |
| Organize | `cdk job tag\|untag <id> tag1,tag2`, `cdk job hide\|unhide <id>` | `hide` is just a tag |
| Pull artifacts | `cdk job sync-outputs <id>` | → `$HOME/cloud-devkit-data/job-outputs/<id>` |
| Profiling | `cdk job trace <id>`, `cdk job xprof <id> [-a]` | → [references/profiling.md](references/profiling.md) |

Hidden but real: `cdk update`, and `cdk job reset-state <id> <state>` (advanced — forces the
CDK State field in MongoDB; only for unwedging a stuck job).

## Rules for running cdk non-interactively

These matter more than anything else in this file when you are the one running the commands.

1. **Parse `-o json`, never the tables.** `usage`, `queue`, `recipe list` and `job list` all
   support it, and it is the only stable contract — table columns are formatted for humans
   and shift between releases. JSON also carries fields the tables drop, notably per-node
   `topology` in `cdk usage`. Everything else (`desc`, `recipe`, `log`) is text-only.
2. **`desc` and `recipe` still emit ANSI colors when piped** — they use chroma, which does
   not check for a TTY, and nothing auto-detects it. Both now take `--no-color`; pass it
   whenever you are parsing. (`sed 's/\x1b\[[0-9;]*m//g'` still works but is no longer
   needed.) The four commands above strip color on pipe, so `grep`/`awk` is safe there.
3. **`log`, `exec` and `attach` resolve the pod and container themselves.** With one
   candidate container you need no flags at all. With several, the command exits **1** and
   prints the valid pairs to stderr, ready to paste back:
   ```
   job j-... has 5 containers, select one with -p and -c:
     -p j-...-prefill-0-0-8zsn -c vllm-tpu-prefill
   ```
   It only prompts interactively when stdin is a TTY, so it can no longer hang or
   silently do nothing under an agent.
4. **Exit codes are meaningful; errors go to stderr.** A failure exits non-zero and writes
   the diagnostic to stderr, leaving stdout empty — so `if ! out=$(cdk ...)` and
   `2>/dev/null` both behave. One exception survives, rule 5.
5. **A `log` read that fails still falls back to a Log Explorer URL on stdout** (`-e` does
   this deliberately). A URL where you expected log text means the read failed, even though
   the command succeeded.
6. **`cdk job desc` is enormous** — it embeds every k8s event for every pod. Pipe to
   `head`, or grep for the field you want, rather than dumping it.
7. **Confirm before `cdk job create` and `cdk job delete`.** Both mutate shared cluster
   state; `create` takes scarce TPU chips from other engineers and `delete` is not
   restricted to your own jobs. Never delete a job you didn't launch without being asked to.
8. **Don't print or commit `~/.cdk.ini`.** It holds the MongoDB connection string with
   credentials. (Its other keys: `cdk_root_dir`, `cdk_job_outputs_dir`.)
9. **Let cdk auto-update.** It checks for a new release on nearly every invocation and
   replaces its own binary in place. That is fine to leave on when scripting: the "a new
   version is found" notice is written only when stdout is a TTY and erased afterwards, so
   it never contaminates piped output. **The one exception is testing a local build** —
   `make build-cli` stamps `VERSION` from `git describe --always`, which is a commit hash
   rather than the literal `dev` that would skip the check, so your fresh `bin/cdk` reverts
   to the published release on first run. Use `NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1` for that case only.

> [!NOTE]
> Verified against `cdk b5bcf4d` (2026-07-31), which is the tip of `cloud-devkit-admin`
> master — the `agent-friendly-cli` work is merged and released. Rules 2 and 5 are the
> workarounds that survived it; rules 1, 3 and 4 describe what it fixed.

## Triage playbook

**"My job isn't starting."**
```bash
cdk job list -A                 # JobStatus: InQueue = waiting on Kueue; Pending = scheduling
cdk usage                       # are there actually free TPU chips?
cdk queue                       # how deep is the backlog, per user
```
`InQueue` + free chips usually means a quota/flavor problem, not a hardware one — check
`cloud-devkit-admin/config/cloud-devkit-jobs/tpu7x-cluster-queue.yaml`. See
[references/capacity.md](references/capacity.md).

**"My job failed."**
```bash
cdk job desc <id> --no-color | grep -A3 -i "reason\|event" | head -40
cdk job log <id>                 # add -p/-c only if it tells you to
cdk job recipe <id> --no-color   # what actually got submitted after templating
```
An *unguarded* missing template variable now aborts the submission, so a job that exists at
all rendered cleanly. The trap that survives is the opposite one: a typo'd `KEY=VALUE` for a
variable the recipe guards with `| default` is silently ignored and you get the default.
Check `cdk job recipe` against what you meant to pass. See
[references/jobs.md](references/jobs.md) for that and for State vs JobStatus.

**"I need the outputs / a profile."**
```bash
cdk job sync-outputs <id>       # required before `cdk job trace`
cdk job xprof <id>              # device-level, needs .xplane.pb in $CDK_OUTPUT_DIR
cdk job list -o wide            # Perfetto trace URLs (go/cdk-perfetto/<id>)
```

## Reference files

- [references/jobs.md](references/jobs.md) — job lifecycle, every flag, templating and
  validation rules, log modes, states.
- [references/capacity.md](references/capacity.md) — reading `cdk usage` and `cdk queue`,
  TPU slice math, tested one-liners.
- [references/recipes.md](references/recipes.md) — writing/registering recipes, the NFS
  fast dev loop, Docker images.
- [references/profiling.md](references/profiling.md) — Perfetto and xprof.

## Two repos

This skill ships in **`cloud-devkit-recipes`** (checked out at `$HOME/cloud-devkit`, which
is cdk's `cdk_root_dir`). Unqualified paths in these files — `recipes.yml`,
`recipes/golden/`, `dockerfiles/`, `lib/src/cdk/` — are in this repo.

The cdk CLI and the cluster config live in a **separate** repo, `cloud-devkit-admin`
(on this VM: `~/data/cloud-devkit-admin`). Paths into it are written with a
`cloud-devkit-admin/` prefix throughout. Note that both repos have a `lib/`, and they are
unrelated: Go here, Python there.

Code of record, all in `cloud-devkit-admin`: `cli/` (commands), `lib/` (k8s, db, logs,
tracegen), `services/job_manager/` (post-completion state machine),
`config/cloud-devkit-jobs/` (Kueue quota). User docs: `docs/content/`, published at
go/cdk-doc.


===== .claude/skills/cdk/references/capacity.md =====
# Cluster capacity: `cdk usage` and `cdk queue`

`cdk usage` prints the whole cluster as a tree: **NodePool → Node → Pod**. It is the answer
to "is there room for my job?" and "who has the TPUs?". Its only flag is `-o default|json`;
it hits the live GKE cluster and MongoDB, takes a few seconds, and needs working `kubectl`
credentials. Colors are stripped when piped, so `grep`/`awk` is safe.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Use `cdk usage -o json` for anything you compute on.** It is released, and it emits raw
> `{requests, allocatable}` per node instead of rounded percentages — plus a per-node
> `topology` field the tree only shows on the NodePool header. The `awk` recipes further
> down still work and are kept for reading by eye, but JSON is both easier and more correct.
>
> ```json
> { "node_pool": "tpu7x-multihost-8chip-2025-1",
>   "name": "gke-tpu-6700501d-8272",
>   "status": "Ready",
>   "topology": "tpu-slice=2x2x2",          // absent on non-TPU nodes
>   "cpu":    { "requests": 220618, "allocatable": 223370 },        // millicores
>   "memory": { "requests": 860446085376, "allocatable": 985587040256 },
>   "tpu":    { "requests": 4, "allocatable": 4 },                  // absent on non-TPU nodes
>   "pods": [ { "name": "...", "status": "Running", "job_id": "j-...",
>               "user": "...", "recipe": "..." } ] }
> ```
>
> `cdk queue -o json` gives `{name, cluster_queue, pending_workloads, admitted_workloads,
> reserving_workloads, consumed_resources{cpu, memory, google.com/tpu, ephemeral-storage}}`.
>
> **JSON does not report cordons** — `status` is the kubelet Ready condition and a
> `Ready,SchedulingDisabled` node still says `"Ready"`. There is no `unschedulable` field
> anywhere in `cli/nodes.go`. Interpretation rule 7 below still applies in full.

Source, in the `cloud-devkit-admin` repo: `cli/nodes.go` (rendering), `lib.K8sGetNodeTree`
in `lib/kubernetes.go` (data collection).

## Anatomy

```
+-- NodePool: tpu7x-singlehost-2025 (tpu-slice=2x2x1)
|   +-- Node: gke-tpu-dff5da07-1qlq (Ready) [CPU: 32.6/223.4 cores (15%), RAM: 901.4/917.9 GiB (98%), TPU: 4/4]
|   |   \-- Pod: j-3c5bf4dd-...-server-0-0-ktrxm (Running) [JobId=j-3c5bf4dd-479b-44ae-a0a1, User=pyc_google_com, Recipe=sglang-...]
|   +-- Node: gke-tpu-dff5da07-kj9z (Ready) [... TPU: 0/4]
|   |   \-- no active CDK jobs
```

- **NodePool** — grouped by the `cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool` label; `tpu-slice=` is the
  GKE TPU topology label.
- **Node** — readiness, then `requests/allocatable` for CPU and RAM, plus `TPU:`/`GPU:`
  chip counts on accelerator nodes.
- **Pod** — only pods in the `cloud-devkit-jobs` namespace, with `JobId`, `User`, `Recipe`
  resolved from MongoDB.

## Interpretation rules

1. **`TPU: X/Y` is the number that matters, not CPU%.** A node at `TPU: 0/4` is free even
   if CPU/RAM look busy; `TPU: 4/4` is fully claimed even at 0% CPU. Free is not the same
   as schedulable — see rule 7.

2. **CPU/RAM percentages count every active pod cluster-wide** — `kube-system` daemonsets,
   `cloud-devkit-services`, other namespaces — not just CDK jobs. That's why an idle node
   still shows ~0.6 cores / 1.4 GiB, and why `default-pool` nodes sit at 83%.

3. **`TPU: 4/4` with "no active CDK jobs" means something outside `cloud-devkit-jobs` holds
   the chips.** Requests sum over all non-terminal pods **including Pending** ones, but only
   `cloud-devkit-jobs` pods are listed. Confirm with:
   ```bash
   kubectl get pods -A --field-selector spec.nodeName=<node> \
     -o custom-columns=NS:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,PHASE:.status.phase
   ```

4. **Terminal pods are listed (dimmed) but consume nothing.** `Succeeded`/`Failed` pods are
   excluded from request totals, so a node can show `TPU: 0/4` and still list a `Succeeded`
   pod. Don't count those as active users.

5. **`JobId=`, `User=unknown`, `Recipe=unknown`** means the pod isn't a CDK-tracked job — a
   hand-made pod (`debug-pod`), a support job (`sj-weights-loader-*`), or a failed DB
   lookup. Don't attribute it to a person.

6. **Free capacity is per-slice, not per-node, on multihost pools.** A `2x2x2` multihost
   pool is 2 nodes × 4 chips = one 8-chip slice and a job needs the whole slice; one free
   node in a busy multihost pool is not usable capacity. `2x2x1` singlehost = 4 chips on one
   node, allocatable individually.

7. **`cdk usage` hides two things that decide schedulability: cordons and per-node
   topology — and JSON only fixes one of them.** Topology *is* in `-o json` per node, so
   that half is solved if you parse JSON; in the tree it appears only on the **NodePool**
   header, so any `grep` over *node* lines (including `grep "TPU: 0/"`) throws it away.
   **Cordons are invisible in both.** A node that `kubectl` reports as
   `Ready,SchedulingDisabled` renders as `(Ready)` in the tree and `"status": "Ready"` in
   JSON, so neither the `grep -v "(Ready)"` one-liner below nor any JSON filter will find
   it — `kubectl` is still required. Both losses push the same direction: `TPU: 0/4`
   overstates available capacity. One sample taken while writing this: 7 nodes at
   `TPU: 0/`, of which 4 were `2x2x2` (wrong topology for a singlehost job), 1 was
   cordoned, and **2** were genuinely usable. Verify with the intersection one-liner below
   before concluding there is room.

## Tested one-liners

### JSON (preferred for anything scripted)

**Free nodes a singlehost job can actually land on** — free chips ∩ right topology ∩ not
cordoned. This is the JSON equivalent of the `awk` version below, and the one to reach for.
The `kubectl` half is not optional: cordons are absent from the cdk output entirely.
```bash
comm -12 \
  <(cdk usage -o json | python3 -c "
import sys, json
for n in json.load(sys.stdin):
    t = n.get('tpu')
    if t and t['requests'] == 0 and n.get('topology') == 'tpu-slice=2x2x1':
        print(n['name'])" | sort) \
  <(kubectl get nodes -o custom-columns='N:.metadata.name,U:.spec.unschedulable' --no-headers \
    | awk '$2!="true"{print $1}' | sort)
```
Run while writing this: 5 nodes in `tpu7x-singlehost-2026` had free chips, 4 survived the
topology and cordon filters — the dropped one was cordoned and looked `Ready` in both the
tree and the JSON.

**Free vs. total TPU nodes per pool**
```bash
cdk usage -o json | python3 -c "
import sys, json, collections
free, tot = collections.Counter(), collections.Counter()
for n in json.load(sys.stdin):
    if not n.get('tpu'): continue
    tot[n['node_pool']] += 1
    if n['tpu']['requests'] == 0: free[n['node_pool']] += 1
for p in sorted(tot): print(f'{p:<34} free {free[p]}/{tot[p]}')"
```

### Text tree (for reading by eye)

**Free TPU nodes** — raw, and overstates capacity; see interpretation rule 7
```bash
cdk usage | grep "TPU: 0/"
```

**Free nodes a singlehost job can actually land on** — free chips ∩ right topology ∩ not
cordoned. Change the topology label to match your recipe's `nodeSelector`.
```bash
comm -12 \
  <(cdk usage | grep -oP 'Node: \K[^ ]+(?= .*TPU: 0/)' | sort) \
  <(kubectl get nodes -l cloud.google.com/gke-tpu-topology=2x2x1 \
      -o custom-columns='N:.metadata.name,U:.spec.unschedulable' --no-headers \
    | awk '$2!="true"{print $1}' | sort)
```
The obvious-looking `-o jsonpath='{range .items[?(@.spec.unschedulable!=true)]}'` is wrong
here: the field is *absent* on schedulable nodes, so the comparison matches nothing and you
get an empty list that reads like "zero capacity" instead of "all of them".

**Why one specific "free" node was passed over**
```bash
kubectl get nodes <node> -o custom-columns='NODE:.metadata.name,TOPO:.metadata.labels.cloud\.google\.com/gke-tpu-topology,CORDONED:.spec.unschedulable'
```

**Free vs. total TPU nodes per pool** (read multihost results in whole slices)
```bash
cdk usage | awk '
/NodePool:/ { pool=$0; sub(/.*NodePool: /,"",pool); sub(/ .*/,"",pool); next }
/Node: / && /TPU: / { total[pool]++; if ($0 ~ /TPU: 0\//) free[pool]++ }
END { for (p in total) printf "%-32s free %d / %d nodes\n", p, free[p]+0, total[p] }' | sort
```

**Who is holding TPU nodes** (skips terminal pods; ×4 chips per node)
```bash
cdk usage | awk '
/Node: / && /TPU: [1-9]/ { node=$0; sub(/.*Node: /,"",node); sub(/ .*/,"",node); next }
/Node: / { node=""; next }
/Pod: / && node!="" && $0 !~ /(Succeeded|Failed)\)/ {
  u=$0; sub(/.*User=/,"",u); sub(/,.*/,"",u); c[u]++ }
END { for (u in c) printf "%-28s %d TPU nodes (%d chips)\n", u, c[u], c[u]*4 }' | sort -k2 -rn
```

**Everything one user is running / non-Ready nodes**
```bash
cdk usage | grep "User=<ldap>"
cdk usage | grep "Node: " | grep -v "(Ready)"
```

## `cdk queue` — the admission side

A table of every Kueue LocalQueue: **Pending / Admitted / Reserving** workload counts plus
consumed CPU, memory, TPU, ephemeral storage. Queues are named `lq-<user>`.

- Pending > 0 means workloads are waiting for admission — the cluster is oversubscribed for
  that flavor, regardless of what `cdk usage` shows.
- Memory and CPU are raw Kueue quantities (`373783m` = millicores; memory in milli-bytes),
  so they read strangely. Compare relative magnitudes rather than parsing units.
- The same person often shows up twice (`lq-jane`, `lq-jane-google-com`) because the queue
  name is derived from `$USER` at submit time and differs between VMs.

## When free chips still won't schedule

There are two independent gates — Kueue **admission**, then kube-scheduler **placement** —
and the fix is different for each. Check which one you are stuck behind before digging into
quota YAML.

### Admitted, but never placed

`JobStatus=Pending` together with `Started / Admitted by clusterQueue tpu7x-cq` in the
events means Kueue already let the job through and the *scheduler* could not place the pod.
That is a node-level problem — topology, cordon, memory — not a quota one. The event says
which, with a per-reason node census:

```bash
cdk job desc <id> --no-color | grep -A2 FailedScheduling
```
```
0/49 nodes are available: 1 node(s) were unschedulable, 12 Insufficient google.com/tpu,
36 node(s) didn't match Pod's node affinity/selector, 5 Insufficient memory.
```

The counts are tags, not a partition — a node can be listed under several reasons, so they
sum past the node total. `didn't match Pod's node affinity/selector` is almost always your
`nodeSelector` topology; `node(s) were unschedulable` is a cordon.

### Never admitted: quota

If the events show no `Admitted by clusterQueue` at all, you are stuck at the first gate.
`cdk usage` is physical capacity; admission is Kueue's call. Quota lives in
`cloud-devkit-admin/config/cloud-devkit-jobs/tpu7x-cluster-queue.yaml` (per-flavor `nominalQuota` under cluster
queue `tpu7x-cq`), and the flavor→nodepool mapping in
`cloud-devkit-admin/config/cloud-devkit-jobs/tpu7x-flavors.yaml`.

**A flavor with no `nominalQuota` entry in the cluster queue can never be admitted to, so
its nodes sit permanently idle no matter what `cdk usage` reports.** At the time of writing
`tpu7x-multihost-8chip-2026` is commented out of the cluster queue, and its two nodes are
correspondingly unused.

Don't read the YAML as the live state. `make k8s-deploy` (in `cloud-devkit-admin`) runs
`kubectl apply -k config`,
which **does not prune** — commenting a ResourceFlavor out of `tpu7x-flavors.yaml` leaves
the object in the cluster. `tpu7x-singlehost-2026` is commented out in the file yet still
exists and carries the largest quota (52 chips) and the heaviest load. Check reality with:

```bash
kubectl get resourceflavors
kubectl get clusterqueue tpu7x-cq \
  -o jsonpath='{range .spec.resourceGroups[*].flavors[*]}{.name}{"\t"}{.resources[0].nominalQuota}{"\n"}{end}'
```


===== .claude/skills/cdk/references/jobs.md =====
# CDK jobs

Source of record, in the `cloud-devkit-admin` repo: `cli/jobs.go`, `cli/main.go` (flag
registration), `lib/db.go` (job states), `services/job_manager/main.go` (post-completion
state machine).

## `cdk job create <recipe> [KEY=VALUE ...]`

| Flag | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--ttl`, `-l` | `600` | Seconds the finished JobSet lingers before k8s GC. **Silently clamped to [300, 3600]** — 300 is the floor so CDK can archive logs; 3600 the ceiling because resources aren't released until deletion. |
| `--tags`, `-t` | — | Comma-separated. Spaces are a fatal error. |
| `--mount-gcs` | `true` | Mounts the job's GCS prefix at `/cdk-outputs` and sets `$CDK_OUTPUT_DIR`. Forced on if the recipe sets `require_gcs_mount: true`. |
| `--log-mode` | `k8s-sync-log` | `k8s-sync-log` \| `gcs-redirect` \| `log-transport` (see below). |
| `--backoff-limit-override` | `true` | Forces `backoffLimit: 0` on replicatedJobs that don't set one, so failed jobs don't silently retry. |

### Templating — the trap that survives

Recipes are Go templates rendered with the `KEY=VALUE` pairs before submission. Two former
traps are now fixed in the CLI: only the positional args after the recipe name are scanned
for `KEY=VALUE` (so `--tags=a` is no longer registered as a variable named `--tags`), and an
**unguarded** `{{ .FOO }}` you forgot to supply aborts the submission — `findUnresolvedVars`
in `cli/jobs.go` catches the rendered `<no value>`, prints the offending lines to stderr and
exits 1.

What remains, and it is the more likely mistake in practice:

> **A typo'd key for a variable the recipe guards with `| default` is silently ignored.**
> The unresolved-variable check skips any template action containing `default`, because such
> an action can never render `<no value>`. So with `{{ .PROMPT_LEN | default "0" }}` in the
> recipe, `cdk job create ttpu-dev PROMPT_LNE=8192` submits happily and runs with
> `PROMPT_LEN=0`. Since the standing advice is to give *every* variable a default, this
> covers essentially every well-written recipe.

There is no validation against a declared parameter list — the recipe never declares one.
The only defence is to read back what was submitted:

```bash
cdk job recipe <id> --no-color | grep -n "PROMPT_LEN" -A1
```

Keep writing variables as `{{ .FOO | default "x" }}` anyway; the custom `default` helper
catches both the missing-key and the empty-string case, and an undefaulted variable is a
submission-time failure rather than a silently wrong run.

### Validation — rejected at submit time

- `serviceAccountName` set anywhere → fatal. CDK assigns the service account itself.
- `hostNetwork: true` → fatal (incompatible with the GCS FUSE mount).
- `restartPolicy` other than `Never` → fatal; empty is rewritten to `Never`.

### What CDK injects

- `terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 180` on every pod spec.
- Label `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name: lq-<$USER with _ → ->`, and the LocalQueue is created
  on demand against cluster queue `tpu7x-cq`.
- Annotation `cloud-devkit.google.com/username: $USER`.
- Per log mode: a preStop hook (180 s for `k8s-sync-log`, 60 s for `gcs-redirect`), a
  pod finalizer `kubernetes.io/custom-debugging-hook` for `k8s-sync-log`, or the label
  `cloud-devkit/log-transport: "true"` for `log-transport`.

Because the queue name is derived from `$USER`, the same person appears under two queues
(`lq-jane` and `lq-jane-google-com`) if their VMs disagree about `$USER`. That's why
`cdk queue` lists near-duplicate rows.

The rendered JobSet is uploaded to GCS at submit time, which is what `cdk job recipe`
reads back later.

## Log modes

| Mode | How logs reach you | Trade-off |
|---|---|---|
| `k8s-sync-log` (default) | job_manager pulls container logs from the k8s API and archives to GCS; a pod finalizer keeps pods around long enough to do it | Reliable, but the finalizer is why a deleted job can hang — that's what `cdk job delete -f` clears |
| `gcs-redirect` | container command is wrapped to write straight to the GCS mount | Requires `--mount-gcs`; no k8s log retention limits |
| `log-transport` | pod is labelled and the log-transport service ships logs out | No preStop hook or wrapper applied |

## `cdk job list`

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `-a` | All users (default: only `$USER`) |
| `-H` | Include `hide`-tagged jobs |
| `-n N` | Most recent N (default 32) |
| `-o wide` | Adds **State** and the Perfetto trace URL |
| `-o debug` | Adds Hostname and the cdk version that submitted (useful for "did an old client do this?") |
| `-o json` | Machine-readable; one object per job with `id, user, hostname, recipe, tags, create_time, complete_time, job_status, state, active, cdk_version, cdk_built_time`. Use this for any scripted filtering — note it carries `state` and `active` that the default table omits, but **not** the Perfetto URL, which only `-o wide` prints. |
| `-t/-u/-r` | Filter by tag / user / recipe |
| `-A` | Only non-terminal jobs |

Behaviours worth knowing:
- Sorted by create time **ascending** — newest rows are at the bottom.
- `-n` truncates *after* filtering, keeping the newest N.
- `-u <user>` implicitly enables all-users; you don't also need `-a`.
- `-A` filters on **JobStatus** terminality (`Succeeded`/`Failed`/`Deleted`), not on State.
  A job that finished executing but is still archiving logs is excluded by `-A` even though
  CDK is still working on it.

## Reading logs

`cdk job log <id> [-p POD -c CONTAINER] [-f] [-e] [-P]`

- **`-p`/`-c` are optional.** `resolvePodAndContainer` fills in whichever you omit: a single
  candidate container needs nothing, and a real choice exits 1 with the valid pairs on
  stderr (it only prompts when stdin is a TTY).
- Routing is decided by **State**, not JobStatus: once State reaches `LogArchiveSucceeded`,
  `TraceGenFailed`, `Finalizing`, `FinalizingFailed`, or `Complete`, logs are read from
  GCS. Before that, cdk shells out to `kubectl logs -n cloud-devkit-jobs`.
- `-f` (follow) is only meaningful pre-archive — after archiving there's nothing to stream.
- `-e` prints a Cloud Logging Log Explorer URL instead of the logs. cdk also falls back to
  this URL automatically whenever a read fails — printed to stdout, mixed in with the log
  output, and this path still succeeds. A URL where you expected logs means something went
  wrong; it is the one failure the exit code does not tell you about.
- `-P` re-executes cdk and pipes into `$PAGER` (default `less -R -S`) — never use this
  non-interactively.

## Job states (`State`, from `cdk job desc` / `list -o wide`)

Driven by `cloud-devkit-admin/services/job_manager/main.go`:

```
Active ──k8s terminal──> ExecutionComplete ──archive logs──> LogArchiveSucceeded
                                    │                              │
                            LogArchiveFailed              gen Perfetto trace
                                    │                              │
                                    │                      TraceGenFailed
                                    ▼                              ▼
                                 Finalizing ──remove pod finalizers──> Complete
                                    └── FinalizingFailed
```
Plus `K8sJobDeleted` when the JobSet is deleted before finishing.

`Finalizing` is where pod finalizers get removed. A job stuck in `Finalizing` or
`FinalizingFailed` is holding pods — that's the case for `cdk job delete -f`, and, as a last
resort, `cdk job reset-state <id> <state>`.

## Other commands

- `cdk job delete <id> [-f]` — deletes the JobSet and marks the DB record deleted. `-f`
  first strips the debug finalizer from every pod so deletion isn't blocked.
- `cdk job desc <id> [--no-color]` — the full MongoDB record as YAML: user, hostname, the
  exact `cmd` that submitted it, tags, State, JobStatus, the jobs/pods/containers tree with
  node names, every k8s event, and CDK's own event log. Colored even when piped — pass
  `--no-color` when parsing.
- `cdk job recipe <id> [--no-color]` — the rendered JobSet as submitted, from GCS. Same
  colour caveat.
- `cdk job tag|untag <id> tag1,tag2` — second positional arg is comma-separated.
  `hide`/`unhide` just add or remove the `hide` tag.
- `cdk job sync-outputs <id>` — syncs GCS outputs to `$HOME/cloud-devkit-data/job-outputs/<id>`
  (configurable via `cdk_job_outputs_dir`). If logs aren't archived yet it also pulls live
  container logs from the k8s API into `logs/<job>/<pod>/<container>.log`.
- `cdk job reset-state <id> <state>` — hidden. Forces the State field in MongoDB and appends
  an audit event. Valid values are in `lib.AllJobStates`.

## Agent-friendly CLI changes (released in `b5bcf4d`, 2026-07-31)

All merged to `cloud-devkit-admin` master and live in the published binary:

- `-o json` on `job list`, `usage`, `queue` and `recipe list`.
- Unresolved *unguarded* template variables abort the submission (see the templating
  section for the defaulted-variable case, which they do not catch).
- Only positional args after the recipe name are scanned for `KEY=VALUE`.
- Pod and container resolve automatically; ambiguity exits 1 listing the valid pairs.
- Failures exit non-zero with diagnostics on stderr.
- `desc` gained `--no-color`.

One prediction did **not** land: neither `desc` nor `recipe` drops color when piped. There
is no TTY detection on that path, so `--no-color` remains mandatory for parsing.


===== .claude/skills/cdk/references/profiling.md =====
# Profiling CDK jobs

Two complementary tools. **Perfetto = host-level, end-to-end, automatic.**
**Xprof = device-level, opt-in, you instrument the capture.**

Source, all in the `cloud-devkit-admin` repo: `lib/tracegen.go`, `cli/jobs.go`,
`services/job_manager/main.go`, `services/xprof_uploader/`, `services/trace_loader/`;
user docs `docs/content/profiling/`.

## Perfetto (host)

Generated automatically. After a job's logs are archived, job_manager parses the logs from
**all** containers and pattern-matches known events into a single trace covering every
process, then hosts it permanently.

```bash
cdk job list -o wide          # PfTraceUrl column → http://go/cdk-perfetto/<job-id>
```

The URL only appears when the job's `PfEventNumber > 0`, i.e. the parser actually matched
events. **No URL means no matching log lines were found — you probably didn't enable the
VLOGs.** Add to the container running the server:

```yaml
- { name: TPU_VMODULE, value: "tpu_pjrt_client=1,pjrt_stream_executor_client=1,tpu_pjrt_compiler_utils=1" }
- { name: TPU_STDERR_LOG_LEVEL, value: "0" }
- { name: VLLM_LOGGING_LEVEL, value: "DEBUG" }
```

With an old libtpu these VLOGs may not exist; copy a newer one in and point
`TPU_LIBRARY_PATH` at it (`gs://cloud-devkit/libs/libtpu/20260521/libtpu.so`).

That covers HLO and MapDmaBuffer events. For application-level spans, use the `cdk.trace`
Python library from `lib/src` in this repo (put it on `PYTHONPATH`) — it writes
structured markers to stdout/stderr that the parser picks up:

```python
from cdk import trace

@trace.trace_func()              # or trace.trace_func("CustomName")
def my_function(): ...

with trace.trace_me("DataProcessing"):   # end event emitted even on exception
    ...

trace.trace_start("Request-1"); trace.trace_end("Request-1")   # across call stacks
```

### Local trace generation

```bash
cdk job sync-outputs <id>     # REQUIRED first — trace gen reads the local sync dir
cdk job trace <id>            # prints the local trace file path and event count
```

`cdk job trace` fails with a confusing error if you skip `sync-outputs`. Note the automatic
pipeline already does this server-side; the local command is for when trace gen failed
(State `TraceGenFailed`) or you want to iterate on parsing.

## Xprof (device)

You implement the capture in your code; CDK only handles distribution. Write the dumps
under `$CDK_OUTPUT_DIR` (requires GCS mounting, on by default). After completion CDK scans
`gs://cloud-devkit/jobs/<id>/outputs` for `*.xplane.pb`.

```bash
cdk job xprof <id>            # prints every link when non-interactive or single-file
cdk job xprof <id> -a         # force all links even at a terminal
```

Links are `go/cdk-xprof?<relative-path>`. **`-a` is no longer required when scripting.** The
picker is now gated on `showAll || len(files) == 1 || !term.IsTerminal(os.Stdin)`, so with
no TTY it prints every link instead of opening a `promptui` selector that could not be
answered. Passing `-a` anyway is harmless and makes the intent explicit. Only the
interactive path copies to the clipboard via an OSC-52 escape sequence, so that noise no
longer reaches captured output.

"No `.xplane.pb` files found" means either the capture never ran or it wrote somewhere
other than `$CDK_OUTPUT_DIR`.

## Choosing

| Question | Tool |
|---|---|
| Where did wall-clock time go across the whole run? | Perfetto |
| Why is disagg prefill/decode coordination slow? | Perfetto (single trace, all processes) |
| What is the TPU actually doing per op? | Xprof |
| Does profiling perturb my benchmark numbers? | Perfetto is lightweight; xprof is not |


===== .claude/skills/cdk/references/recipes.md =====
# Recipes and the development loop

A recipe is a Kubernetes **JobSet** template plus a registration entry. JobSet (not plain
Job) is used because CDK workloads are multi-pod: multi-host TPU training, or disaggregated
serving with separate prefill/decode replica groups.

Source, in the `cloud-devkit-admin` repo: `lib/recipes.go`, `cli/jobs.go`; user doc
`docs/content/write-a-new-recipe.md`.

## Where recipes live

In **this** repo — `cloud-devkit-recipes`, which cdk reads as `cdk_root_dir` (default
`$HOME/cloud-devkit`, overridable in `~/.cdk.ini`).

```
$HOME/cloud-devkit/          # this repo
├── recipes.yml              # the registry cdk reads
├── recipes/golden/          # stable, peer-reviewed, shared
├── recipes/experimental/    # your own work in progress
├── dockerfiles/             # base, vllm-torchax, bench, eval, ...
└── lib/src/cdk/trace.py     # the cdk.trace Python tracing helpers
```

`cdk recipe list` reads `recipes.yml` and prints name / owner / YAML path. Adding a recipe
means editing that file — there is no `cdk recipe add`.

## Registration entry

```yaml
- name: "my-awesome-recipe"          # what users type in `cdk job create`
  owner: "your_ldap_google_com"
  k8s_file: "recipes/experimental/my-awesome-recipe/values.yml"   # relative to repo root
  require_gcs_mount: true            # optional; forces --mount-gcs on
```

## Template contract

Go templating, rendered against the `KEY=VALUE` pairs from the command line:

```yaml
args:
  - |
    mkdir -p $CDK_OUTPUT_DIR
    while [ $elapsed -lt {{ .SLEEPTIME | default "60" }} ]; do ...
```

**Always use `| default "..."`.** The template is parsed with `missingkey=zero`, so an
unguarded `{{ .FOO }}` you never supplied renders as `<no value>` — the CLI now catches that
and aborts the submission rather than shipping broken YAML, but a failed submit is still a
wasted round trip. The custom `default` helper handles both the missing-key and
empty-string cases.

The flip side, and the one the CLI cannot catch: **a defaulted variable silently swallows a
typo'd key.** `PROMPT_LNE=8192` against `{{ .PROMPT_LEN | default "0" }}` runs with `0` and
no warning, because the check skips any action containing `default`. Read back the render
before trusting a run — see [jobs.md](jobs.md) for the details.

`$CDK_OUTPUT_DIR` is injected when GCS mounting is on (normally `/cdk-outputs`, backed by a
GCS bucket). Anything written there is persisted, archived, and scanned for `.xplane.pb`
profiles.

## Hard restrictions (enforced at submit, fatal)

1. No `serviceAccountName` — CDK assigns it.
2. No `hostNetwork: true` — breaks the GCS FUSE mount.
3. `restartPolicy` must be `Never` (empty is rewritten; anything else is rejected).
4. Containers must actually exit, or TTL cleanup never runs and the TPUs stay held.

CDK also forces `terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 180` and `backoffLimit: 0` (unless the
recipe sets its own and `--backoff-limit-override=false`).

## Testing a new recipe

```bash
cdk recipe list | grep my-awesome-recipe     # confirm registration parsed
cdk job create my-awesome-recipe MY_VAR=test-value
cdk job recipe <id> --no-color               # verify the render before debugging the run
```

## Fast iteration: NFS instead of image rebuilds

The prebuilt images (`vllm-torchax`, `vllm-torchtpu`) clone `vllm` / `tpu-inference` into
`/root/cloud-devkit/` and `pip install -e .` them. Mounting your working copy over those
paths means containers run your latest edits with no rebuild:

```yaml
volumes:
  - name: vllm-nfs
    nfs: { server: <FILESTORE-IP>, path: /workspace/vllm }
containers:
  - name: vllm-server
    volumeMounts:
      - { name: vllm-nfs, mountPath: /root/cloud-devkit/vllm }
```

Set up once: create a Filestore instance, `mount -t nfs <IP>:/workspace /mnt/workspace` on
your CPU VM, clone repos there. Full walkthrough in `cloud-devkit-admin/docs/content/dev-guide.md`.

The resulting loop: edit on NFS → `cdk job create` → `cdk job log <id> -f`
→ `cdk job exec <id>` to poke at a live container → repeat. Add `-p`/`-c` only when the
job has more than one container and cdk asks you to.

## Docker images

Built from **this** repo's Makefile:

```bash
make push-vllm-torchax
make push-vllm-torchax TPU_COMMIT_HASH=<sha> VLLM_COMMIT_HASH=<sha>
make build-vllm-torchtpu     # needs `gh auth login` AND `gcloud auth login` first
```

Images land in `us-central1-docker.pkg.dev/cloud-tpu-inference-test/vllm-tpu-rdna/`.

The `cloud-devkit-admin` repo has a separate Makefile that builds the CLI and services:
`make build-cli`, `make install-cli`, `make cli-deploy` (publishes the binary that clients
auto-update to), `make k8s-deploy`, `make docs-serve`.


===== README.md =====
# Cloud Devkit

This project helps you to run cloud jobs in a GKE cluster. This project targets at:

* Allowing engineers to do dev work in a GKE cluster with shared TPU/GPU resources.
* Allowing engineers to easily share the recipes for all tests.

Please go to [go/cdk-doc](http://go/cdk-doc) for more details.


